  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026 \n\nSouth Texas remains under west/southwesterly flow aloft as the\nridging influence pushes into the Southeastern US. Meanwhile, a \nsurface low pressure over the northern Gulf will linger through \nthe remainder of the work week. This means that northeasterly \nsurface winds will continue to pull in some of that more moderated\ncontinental air mass into South Texas (as seen in forecast PWATS \nwhich will moderate from 0.5-0.6\" to 0.7-1.0\" values). This will \nresult in comfortable 70 degree temperatures this afternoon with \nlows closer to the upper 50s to low 60s Thursday morning. The \nnorth/northeast flow will be strong enough to keep the offshore \nGulf waters (20-60 nm offshore) under a Small Craft Advisory from \nnoon through midnight. Closer to shore, persistent north-\nnortheasterly flow will keep wave periods high (9-10 seconds). \nThis will keep the rip current risk at moderate and will also be \nthe driving force for wave runup resulting in another period of \ncoastal flooding during the upcoming high tide cycles through at \nleast early Thursday morning. Additionally, higher tides are \nmaking for difficult driving in favorite fishing spots around the \nbays/estuaries and thus they also remain in this coastal flooding.\n\nMeanwhile, a deep trough across the Western US will continue to\nbring several disturbances through that region before pushing into \nthe Rockies Saturday, clipping Texas Saturday night and into \nSunday. Concurrently, the subtropical jet will once again ramp up \nas the trough moves into the Great Plains. This will advect upper-\nlevel Pacific moisture into South Texas combined with the return \nof onshore flow at the surface.\n\nThe next frontal system will really be the avenue that squeezes\nout the added moisture in the form of isolate showers in the warm \nsector before the frontal passage Sunday. While the timing of the \nfront is still in the works, this will bring back 50s for highs to\nstart Thanksgiving week. Latest global ensembles continue to show\na more potent cold front moving through South Texas Tuesday \n(though much like the first front, timing is low confidence as of \nthis writing). While the GEFS has the signal for rain across South\nTexas with this passage, future model runs will fine tune the \ntiming of the front and the amount of rainfall South Texas can see\nout of it.",
